1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:05,310
Twelve simulated agents at Harborline Cooperative, a fictional co-op.

2
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:11,410
Policy as code, a cost board, and a gate that can go red. Offline. No model
call.

3
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:20,810
Fleet policy on inventory and prompts, dated October seventeenth twenty
twenty-six: pass, twelve agents, zero violations.

4
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:26,720
The demo also writes a static board from two thousand sixteen simulated events
and three planted alerts.

5
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:34,330
Invariant eval: three YAML cases, thirty-two property trials, zero breaches.

6
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:44,280
Forced regression on a bad refund event: three breaches, exit one. Harmful
transfer, cost over cap, and an unapproved external write.

7
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:46,980
That red exit is the proof the gate can fail.

8
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:53,000
Use it to keep owners, caps, and human gates in YAML and in C-I.

9
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:59,910
Not covered: answer quality, a live vendor call, or a real spend finding.
Board numbers are simulated.

10
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:06,920
Run python -m governance_board demo after install. Cost of the lab path: zero.

